# Line endings are normalised to LF in the repository and on checkout.
#
# Without this file, a clone on a machine with `core.autocrlf=true` -- which is
# git's default on Windows installs -- rewrites every tracked text file to CRLF
# on the way out, while `git status` stays clean because the stored blobs are
# still LF. That silently breaks anything that hashes a tracked file byte for
# byte. It happened on 2026-09-21: `src/server/securityHeaders.test.ts`
# recomputes the SHA-256 of index.html's inline theme script and compares it to
# THEME_SCRIPT_HASH in `src/server/securityHeaders.ts`, and CRLF changed the
# digest. One test failed -- but the real damage was in the build, where
# `dist/index.html` no longer matched the CSP the server sends, so the theme
# script would have been blocked in production and the flash of light theme it
# exists to prevent would have come back, throwing nothing. CI never sees this:
# `.github/workflows/checks.yml` runs on Linux, where autocrlf is off.
#
# `eol=lf` is deliberate rather than `text=auto` alone: auto-detection decides
# what is text, but the working-tree ending still follows the local config, so
# only an explicit eol pins it on every machine.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary files git must never inspect or convert.
*.png  binary
*.ico  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.repx binary
